<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">South African Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South African Party was a political party founded in 1911 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>, whose leaders governed the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> from 1910 to 1924. It sought reconciliation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and English-speaking South Africans within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and absorbed the Unionist Party in 1920. In 1934 it merged with Hertzog's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> to form the United Party.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of the South African party that won the 1910 South African general election under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a>. On 14 June 1910, before that election, Botha called at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>'s Opera House for a South African National Party, but the party was formally created only in November 1911 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>. It brought together mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> parties: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/het-volk">Het Volk</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaner-bond">Afrikaner Bond</a> and the South African Party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orangia-unie">Orangia Unie</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a>.</p><p>The Cape party of that name had been launched by William Schreiner, a former attorney-general under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, to oppose what he called the personal domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. It offered a more moderate platform than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaner-bond">Afrikaner Bond</a> and favoured more peaceful relations with neighbouring states, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>. In the end the Afrikaner Bond lent its support to form a new government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its main early opponent was the Unionist Party, which supported similar policies but was more English-speaking and instinctively pro-British. The party became more solid after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a> and the more radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> nationalists left it and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. Discontent with its economic policies grew in the hard times of the early 1920s and culminated in a general strike in 1922, which a combination of military intervention and negotiation brought to an end.</p><p>The memory of the strike remained when the government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a> faced the 1924 South African general election, which it lost to a coalition of the National and Labour parties. The party stayed in opposition with its Unionist allies until the unrest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> led Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">Barry Hertzog</a> to form a coalition government with it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 5 December 1934 the coalition partners merged into the United South African National Party, more commonly known as the United Party. A hardline nationalist faction refused to accept the merger from the start, and the remaining nationalists withdrew from the United Party in 1939. What remained was essentially the old South African Party under a new name, though the United Party name was kept.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South African Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Botha, who formally founded the <b>South African Party</b> in 1911, became the first prime minister, and the Union joined Canada, Australia and New Zealand as a self-governing dominion of the British Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
